 'There is a blank space at the bottom of every proposal which interests Habib Rahman. It is where somebody must eventually sign. The conference has filled many sheets with possible futures. Who is prepared to withhold a signature when a proposal should not be built?',
 'This year his contract as secretary of the Delhi Urban Art Commission has been terminated. He had opposed official proposals, including putting a Gandhi statue beneath the empty imperial canopy at India Gate and placing public conveniences where they would obstruct the southern entrance to Jama Masjid. A commission intended to scrutinise the city had encountered the inconvenience of scrutiny.',
 'Rahman comes to the island with the experience of building public housing and institutions for an independent India. He has worked with restricted budgets, urgent needs and the conviction that modern architecture should serve ordinary life. He does not regard a large public undertaking as necessarily an enemy. He knows how much depends on who can question it.',
 'At the transport table, his intervention takes the form of a space left on the route plan. Before the line is approved, the people whose ground it crosses must be able to object. The objection must have somewhere to go, and the person hearing it must possess more than a ceremonial chair. A beautiful diagram offers little protection if every decision has already been made.',
 'The finance workshop adds a question to its draft constitution: can the person entrusted with examination refuse the project without losing the means to examine the next one? It is a less attractive sentence than the prospectus title. For the moment, nobody volunteers to remove it.'
